{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is running a cobbler orchestrator \"stitch\" task #5247, which requires implementing srd020-echo R2.1-R2.4 (escape sequence interpretation features for the `echo` command). The specific requirements are:\n   - R2.1: `-e` must enable interpretation of backslash escape sequences (`\\\\`, `\\a`, `\\b`, `\\c`, `\\e`, `\\f`, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\v`, `\\0NNN` octal, `\\xHH` hex)\n   - R2.2: `\\c` with `-e` terminates output immediately (no further chars, no trailing newline)\n   - R2.3: `-E` disables escape interpretation (default behavior; explicit `-E` is a no-op when `-e` not given)\n   - R2.4: When both `-e` and `-E` are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence\n   \n   Files to modify: `cmd/echo/main.go` and `cmd/echo/echo_test.go`. Acceptance criteria require matching `gecho` reference binary behavior, successful compilation, passing tests, and clean `go vet`.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Differential testing via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` comparing Go binary against GNU `gecho` reference\n   - SIGPIPE handling via `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - GNU echo flag parsing: flags are clusters of `n`, `e`, `E` characters; unrecognized flags are literal arguments\n   - Backslash escape sequence interpretation with `\\c` causing immediate output truncation\n   - `LC_ALL=C` environment for locale-independent testing\n   - Go module at `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils`\n   - Git is managed externally by orchestrator - NO git commands allowed\n   - Write all code before building/testing (no incremental compile-fix loops)\n   - SPDX copyright header required on all Go files\n   - Function size limit: 40 lines max\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **docs/specs/software-requirements/srd020-echo.yaml**\n     - Source of truth for all echo requirements (R1-R4 groups)\n     - R2 group (lines 31-40) defines escape sequence interpretation behavior\n     - Non-goals: no --help/--version, no XSI-compliant behavior, no stdin reading\n   \n   - **cmd/echo/main.go** (existing, to be modified)\n     - Already contains a complete implementation including escape handling\n     - Header comment says \"Implements srd020-echo R1.1-R1.4, R3.1-R3.3\" — needs updating to include R2.1-R2.4\n     - `main()` function (lines 14-56): flag parsing, escape mode toggle, output assembly\n     - `isFlag()` function (lines 60-70): validates flag clusters contain only n/e/E chars\n     - `interpretEscapes()` function (lines 72-151): processes all escape sequences including `\\c` truncation, `\\0NNN` octal, `\\xHH` hex\n     - Key code pattern for escape mode: `escapeMode` starts false (line 19), toggled by 'e'/'E' in flag parsing (lines 31-34), last flag wins (R2.4)\n     - Full existing code:\n       ```go\n       // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n       // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n       \n       // Implements srd020-echo R1.1-R1.4, R3.1-R3.3.\n       package main\n       \n       import (\n           \"os\"\n           \"strings\"\n           \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\"\n       )\n       \n       func main() {\n           sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n           args := os.Args[1:]\n           noNewline := false\n           escapeMode := false\n           i := 0\n           for i < len(args) {\n               arg := args[i]\n               if !isFlag(arg) { break }\n               for _, ch := range arg[1:] {\n                   switch ch {\n                   case 'n': noNewline = true\n                   case 'e': escapeMode = true\n                   case 'E': escapeMode = false\n                   }\n               }\n               i++\n           }\n           operands := args[i:]\n           output := strings.Join(operands, \" \")\n           truncated := false\n           if escapeMode {\n               output, truncated = interpretEscapes(output)\n           }\n           if !noNewline && !truncated { output += \"\\n\" }\n           _, err := os.Stdout.WriteString(output)\n           if err != nil { os.Exit(1) }\n       }\n       ```\n     - `interpretEscapes()` handles: `\\\\`, `\\a`, `\\b`, `\\c` (returns true for truncated), `\\e`, `\\f`, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\v`, `\\0NNN` (octal 1-3 digits), `\\xHH` (hex 1-2 digits), and unknown sequences pass through as literal `\\` + char\n\n   - **cmd/echo/echo_test.go** (existing, to be modified)\n     - Uses `testutils.BuildBinary` and `exec.LookPath(\"gecho\")` with graceful skip\n     - Already contains 34 test cases covering R1 and R2 requirements\n     - Tests include: no-args, single/multiple args, -n flag, unrecognized flags, -e with all escape sequences, -E, -e/-E precedence, combined -ne\n     - Full existing test code available (86 lines)\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet — only file reads were performed before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - The key observation is that the existing code in `cmd/echo/main.go` already appears to fully implement R2.1-R2.4. The `interpretEscapes()` function handles all required escape sequences, `\\c` truncation works correctly, `-E` is the default (escapeMode=false), and last-flag-wins is handled by sequential processing.\n   - The main gap is that the file header comment only claims \"R1.1-R1.4, R3.1-R3.3\" and doesn't mention R2.1-R2.4.\n   - The test file also already has coverage for R2 requirements but may benefit from additional edge case tests.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The full stitch task description with role, repository_files, project_context (vision, architecture, specifications, roadmap, analysis, requirement_states), execution_constitution, go_style_constitution, task description, constrain